== Caring for Each Other’s Souls

=== §. 18. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual prayers.]

The #emph[matter] with which the mutual provident care of husbands and wives ought to be concerned is, in general, the #emph[good] of one another: that each of them should do for the other what Solomon applies in particular to a wife, namely, “good and not harm all #footnote[Prov 31:12.] the days of” their life. Now the good of man extends to his #emph[soul, body, good name, and goods.]

A general duty tending to the good of all these is #emph[prayer.] #footnote[Prayer a mutual duty. 1 Pet 3:7.] Peter requires such conduct of man and wife toward one another that their prayers may “not be hindered.” By this he takes it for granted that prayer is a mutual duty which one owes to the other; and Isaac performed this duty for his wife.#footnote[Gen 25:21.] In this, man and wife may be helpful to each other in all things needful to either of them: for it is the means which God in wisdom #footnote[Matt 7:7. Jas 5:16.] has sanctified for obtaining every needful blessing for ourselves or others. Many count it only a slight duty and of little use; but the truth is that to perform it rightly, in truth and faith, is both difficult in the doing and powerful in the effect. It is the best duty that one can perform for another, and the one that should least be neglected. We heard before that Isaac prayed for his wife; and to show the good he did her by it, it is noted that “the #smallcaps[Lord] heard his prayer,” so that she, being #footnote[Gen 25:21.] “barren” before, by that means “conceived.” All the medicine in the world could not have done her so much good. #footnote[πάντοτε. Luke 18:1.] “At all times,” therefore, #footnote[ἀδιαλείπτως. 1 Thess 5:17.] “without ceasing,” this duty is to be performed. Whenever man and wife make any prayer, they must be mindful of one another in it. Yes, and they must often purposely take occasion to make prayers specially for one another, and that both when apart and also in each other’s presence.

This latter especially concerns the husband, who is like a priest to his wife, and ought to be her mouth to God when the two of them are together. Yet I do not doubt that the wife may pray in her husband’s presence when the two of them are alone, either for #emph[trial] (so that he may know her ability and gift in that kind) or for #emph[help] (if the wife is much better able to perform that duty than the man is, as many wives are). Not without cause, therefore, have I counted this among the common mutual duties.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of the things for which husbands and wives are to pray alone.]

There are various needful blessings which husbands and wives are to pray for that belong only to themselves, and are most fitting to be mentioned in private prayer between themselves, such as

1\. That as the two of them are #emph[one flesh,] so they may also be #emph[one spirit:] that their hearts may be as one, knit together by a true, spiritual, marital love; always delighting in one another, ever helpful to one another, and ready with all willingness and cheerfulness to perform all those duties which they owe to one another.

2\. That their marriage bed may be sanctified; and that, as it is by God’s ordinance, so it may remain to them, through their right use of it, a bed “undefiled.” There is nothing else for which mutual prayer in private between man and wife is more needful; and all the more because of the natural heat of lust which is in most people. If this is not assuaged by prayer (the best means for that purpose), it may prove a defilement #footnote[#emph[Intemperans in coniugio quid aliud nisi quidam adulter vxoris est? Aug. ex Ambr. contr. Iulian. lib.] 2. \[What is a man who is immoderate in marriage but a kind of adulterer with his own wife?\]] of the undefiled bed, and man and wife may become adulterers to one another. As other things, so this also is “sanctified by the word … and prayer.” The #emph[word] gives a warrant and direction for its use; #emph[prayer] both seasons it and obtains a blessing on it.

3\. That they may have children, and such as may be heirs of salvation and live in this world to their own and others’ good: that they may be comely and well-proportioned children, not idiots in understanding, nor monsters in bodily shape, nor yet wicked and infamous in their lives. That could not but be a grief to their parents, and might also open the mouths of the wicked against them.

4\. That God would give them a sufficiency of this world’s goods, and other good means to nourish, nurture and settle their children well; and enough for the support of their family, and of the condition in which God sets them.

5\. That such needful gifts and graces as are lacking in either of them may be worked in them, and such vices and infirmities as they are subject to may be corrected.

These and many other such things give man and wife occasion in a special way to pray for one another, and with one another.

=== §. 20. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ hateful curses and wishes against one another.]

Contrary to that holy and heavenly duty of prayer are those dreadful and hellish imprecations and curses which commonly pass out of the mouths of many husbands and wives against one another (and that many times on very slight occasions), cursing the day that they ever knew one another, and wishing that one of them had been under the ground before they came together. These and similar curses are most hateful in any man’s mouth, but more than most hateful in the mouths of man and wife against one another.

Many who for outward shame refrain from belching out such rotten, stinking speech make little conscience of wishing the same in their hearts. If a husband is at all harsh, and a wife shrewish; or if through sickness or some other such occasion they seem burdensome to each other; or if some dislike of the one arises in the heart of the other; or if their hearts are set on others; or if the survivor is to carry away the goods and lands, their hearts will be filled with a thousand wishes for one another’s death. Yes, many times those who have very good husbands or wives, without any show of reason (only through an inward corruption of their heart and the malicious prompting of Satan, not recognizing their own good), are ready to wish they were in heaven. By this they make a pretense of the eternal bliss of the one for whom they wish it, whereas in truth their only desire is to be loosed and freed from them. God often deals with such wicked wishers (and so shows how hateful such wishes are to him). For sometimes, according to their wish, he takes away good husbands and wives from those who are evil; and when they are gone he makes their loss so keenly felt that those ungodly wishers repent of their rash wishes (as we say) in every vein of their heart. Yes, to make their misery all the greater, he gives them such sour and perverse husbands and wives in the place of those good ones (for a better seldom comes) that they are forced with many deep sighs and groans to wish (but all in vain) their former wives and husbands #footnote[Quam chara sint carendo intelligunt. Cic. in Orator.] alive again, and so to prove the proverb true, #emph[A good thing is not so well recognized by enjoying it as by lacking it.] Sometimes again God in anger crosses their wishes, and first takes away those who wished the death of others; or else he prolongs the life of both, to #footnote[Amicitiae quid haberent voluptatis carendo magis intellexi quàm fruendo. \[I have understood what pleasure friendships held more by lacking them than by enjoying them.\] Idem in oration. post red.] their greater vexation.

=== §. 21. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ neglect of mutual prayer.]

The very neglect of mutual prayer by husbands and wives for each other is also a sin contrary to the duty of prayer named above. If all who are guilty of it were as well known to man as to God, how many more unkind husbands and wives, careless of one another’s good, would be noted than are now! Rare are those husbands and wives who have their set times to pray alone together, if they ever pray for one another at all. Though in outward courtesies they may seem very kind, and in the outward things of this world very provident, yet if they do not pray for one another, they are neither kind nor provident. Hearty, fervent, frequent prayer is the greatest token of kindness, and the best part of provident care, that there can be.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual care for one another’s salvation.]

From the general duty of #emph[prayer,] which is profitable for all things, I come to the particular branches of a man and wife’s #footnote[#emph[Admonendi sunt coniuges vt exhortantes inuicem saluent.] \[Married people are to be admonished to save one another by exhorting one another.\] #emph[Greg. in Past. part.] 3. #emph[Adm.] 28.] mutual provident care. And I will begin first with what is to be sought first, #emph[the good of one another’s soul.] The apostle suggests that this is a thing to be sought after where he says, “How do you know, wife, whether you will#footnote[1 Cor 7:16.] SAVE your husband? Or how do you know, husband, whether you will SAVE your wife?” Peter#footnote[1 Pet 3:1.] charges wives to do their best to “win” their husbands; and Paul#footnote[Eph 5:25.] sets before husbands the pattern of Christ’s love, which had special regard #footnote[Of the particular duty of husbands in this respect, see Treatise 4, §. 47.] to the soul and its salvation. So this is a mutual duty belonging to them both, which Peter further implies where he calls them “fellow heirs of the grace of life.”

It is the greatest good that one can possibly do for another, to be a means of furthering his salvation. And there is #footnote[1 Pet 3:7 συγκληρονόμοι χάριτος ζωῆς.] nothing that can more soundly and firmly knit the heart of one to another than to be a means of it.

=== §. 23. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ care to win one another, when one of them is not called.]

So that the salvation of the soul may be better brought about, regard must be had to the present and particular condition of husband or wife. If one is a #emph[believer] and the other is not, the believer must use every means possible to draw the other to believe also. If both are believers, their mutual care must be to edify one another in their most holy faith.

For the first, it is the main aim of Peter’s exhortation to #footnote[1 Pet 3:1.] believing wives about their conduct, to draw their unbelieving husbands to the true faith. His phrase (“they #footnote[ἵνα κερδηθήσονται.] may be won,” or gained), as it refers in general to the salvation of their souls, so in particular it refers to their first conversion. Now if this duty belongs to a wife, much more does it belong to a husband, who is appointed a #emph[head] to his wife, and a #emph[savior.] To this end Paul advises believing husbands and wives who #footnote[Eph 5:23. 1 Cor 7:12, 13] are married to unbelievers to live with them.

For what a woeful thing it is that two who in this world #footnote[A woeful thing that #emph[one flesh] should be in heaven and hell. Luke 17:34.] are so closely linked together as to make #emph[one flesh] should in the world to come be as far separated from one another as heaven is from hell! This indeed will happen to many: for Christ has expressly foretold that of “two” who were “in one bed” together (and who are more fitly described by this phrase than man and wife, who are most usually called bedfellows?) “one will be taken” (to mercy and glory), “and the other left” (forsaken, left to endless and unrelieved torture and torment). But though it is foretold that it will turn out this way with many a couple, yet our care must be, and that with our utmost power, #footnote[Means of conversion the best cause of love.] to prevent it, both in ourselves and in our bedfellow.

If it pleases the Lord to give such a blessing to the endeavor of a husband or wife as to make it a means of the conversion of their bedfellow, then the party converted will both love the other wholeheartedly and also heartily bless God (as there is just cause) that they were ever so closely linked together.

This duty of winning one another is to be applied to those who are married not only to outright infidels, but also to papists or other such idolaters, to atheists or any other profane persons, to heretics, separatists, schismatics, or any who do not believe rightly.

=== §. 24. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ edifying one another.]

The second duty tending to the #emph[soul’s salvation] is that two believers, being married together, endeavor mutually to build one another up more and more. #footnote[1 Thess 5:11.] One Christian owes this duty to another: much more do man and wife. “See to it,”#footnote[Heb 12:15.] says the apostle, “that no one falls away from the grace of God.” If no one, then neither wife nor husband.

A spiritual edifying of one another is the best use which #footnote[Spiritual edification a main end of the marriage union.] we can make (and ought to make) of those “joints”#footnote[Col 2:19.] and “bonds” by which we are knit to one another. By virtue of them “the body”#footnote[Eph 4:16.] (namely the mystical body of Christ) “grows and builds itself up,” and “grows as God causes it to grow.”#footnote[Col 2:19.] Now since the bond of marriage is the firmest of all, and the one by which we are most closely knit together, by virtue of what bond should we edify one another, if not by virtue of the #emph[marriage bond?]

=== §. 25. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ hindering sin in one another.]

Two things are required for spiritual edification.

One concerns the #emph[hindrances] to growth in grace.

The other its #emph[helps.]

The #emph[hindrances] to grace are all kinds of sins. Sin is to grace as water is to fire: it slakes its heat, and if it is poured on without stint, it will put it out entirely. Because of this there ought to be a mutual care in husbands and wives both to #emph[prevent] sin before it is committed, and also to make what #emph[redress] they can after it is committed.

That it is a mutual duty for husbands and wives, as far #footnote[Man and wife to prevent sin in one another.] as they can, to prevent sin in one another is evident from the reason which the apostle uses to keep them from depriving one another, in these words, “so that Satan will not tempt you#super[+].” #footnote[1 Cor 7:5.] For out of the aim and matter of those words this general doctrine may be gathered: #emph[husbands and wives ought to take care to keep one another from the temptations of Satan,] that is, from sin, to which all his temptations tend. Rebekah#footnote[Gen 27:6, #emph[etc.]] performed the duty of a good wife in keeping Isaac from blessing Esau; if he had done it, he would have sinned against #footnote[Gen 25:23.] God’s express word. Though she failed in the manner of doing it, yet her end was good.

As the love they owe to one another, so the care which they #footnote[By preventing sin in each other, they keep judgments from themselves.] ought to have for themselves requires as much. For sin provokes God’s wrath, his wrath sends down vengeance, and the vengeance which falls on the husband can hardly miss the wife, nor that which falls on the wife miss the husband; and that because of their close union. Though it does not fall on both their heads, yet it cannot but greatly affect, and even afflict, the party who escapes. The wives of those rebels who were #footnote[Num 16:27, 32.] swallowed up alive in the wilderness perished in the same way as their husbands. For those who are as close as husbands and wives, and do not do what they can to prevent one another’s sins, make themselves accessories to them.

For the better carrying out of this duty, husbands and wives #footnote[Direction for preventing sin.] must be watchful over one another, and observe what sins either of them is given to, or what occasions are offered to draw either of them into sin. If either of them is hot-tempered or prone to sudden anger, the other must endeavor to take away all occasions of offense. And if both should be touchy and quick to wrath, when the one sees the other moved first, the party whose anger is not yet stirred ought all the more to be settled and composed to all meekness and patience, so that the whole household is not set on fire by both being provoked together. If either of them is given to drunkenness, covetousness or any other sin, the other ought by wise and gentle persuasion to keep them (as much as they can) from those sins. Yes, they may also get others who are discreet and able to dissuade them, or use whatever other good means they can for that purpose.

=== §. 26. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ redressing sin in one another.]

When either husband or wife has fallen into any sin, it is a mutual duty for the other to use whatever remedy there may be for that sin: as if one of them were wounded, the other must take care to heal that wound. Abigail#footnote[1 Sam 25.] performed her duty in this kind when, after she had heard what churlish treatment her husband gave to David’s servants, she hurried to carry plenty of provisions to David, and humbled herself before him, and so moved David to assuage his wrath. Yes, she also took a fitting time to tell her husband his fault, and the danger into which he brought himself by it.

More directly, and with better success, Jacob redressed the superstition, or rather idolatry, of his wife Rachel, as may be gathered by comparing Gen 31:19, 34 with Genesis 35:2, 4.

A brother in general must not allow sin to lie on his brother; #footnote[Lev 19:17.] much less may husband or wife allow it to lie on the other.

“You must not harbor hatred against your brother” (says the law) “and allow #footnote[It is a branch of hatred to allow sin to lie on anyone.] sin to lie on him.” To do this, then, is a token and fruit of hatred. If a husband should see his wife, or a wife her husband, lying in the fire or the water, ready to be burned or drowned, and not give their best help to pull them out, might they not justly be thought to hate them? But sin is like fire and water, which will burn and drown men in perdition.

This duty may be performed by meek instructions, pithy persuasions, gentle reproofs; yes, and by the help of some #footnote[Matt 18:16.] good minister, or other discreet and faithful friend.

=== §. 27. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ furthering the growth of grace in each other.]

So much for preventing and redressing the hindrances of grace. To this must be added a furthering of its growth, which man and wife must mutually endeavor to bring about in one another. The care which Elkanah had to take his #footnote[1 Sam 1:4. #emph[etc.]] wives with him to the tabernacle of the Lord year by year shows that his desire was to uphold them in the fear of God. Yes, the gifts and portions which he used to give them at that time imply the care he had to encourage them to keep on serving the Lord. It was without question the main end at which the Shunammite aimed in #footnote[2 Kgs 4:10.] providing lodging for the prophet, that both she and her husband might be built up in grace.

This duty may be better carried out by the following means. #footnote[How growth in grace may be furthered.]

1\. By taking notice of the beginning and the least measure of grace, and approving of it.

2\. By frequent conversation about the things that concern it: mutually putting questions to one another about them, and answering them.

3\. By their mutual practice and example: making themselves a pattern of piety to each other.

4\. By performing exercises of religion together, such as praying, singing psalms, reading the word, and the like.

5\. By maintaining holy and religious exercises in the family. #footnote[#emph[Nemo magister ita persuadere poterit quemadmodum vxor. Chrys. hom.] 19. 1 Cor 7.] Though this duty belongs especially to the husband, yet the wife must remind her husband of it if he forgets it, and stir him up if he is reluctant. So did the good Shunammite, 2 Kgs 4:9, 10. No man’s persuasion in this kind can prevail so much with a man as his wife’s.

6\. By stirring up one another to go to the house of God, #footnote[Maritum eo adducito vt sanctorum dierum rationem habeat. \[Lead your husband to have regard for the holy days.\] Greg. Naz. ad Olymp.] to hear the word, partake of the sacrament, and conscientiously perform all the parts of God’s public worship.

There is great need that husbands and wives should endeavor to further the growth of grace in each other, because we are all so prone to fall away and grow cold, just as #footnote[1\. Great need of it.] water does if the fire goes out and more fuel is not put under it. #footnote[2\. Husbands and wives may be very helpful to one another in this.] And of all others, husbands and wives may be most helpful in this, because they can soonest spot the beginning of decline through their close and continual familiarity together.

=== §. 28. #emph[Of the sins of husbands and wives contrary to a mutual care for one another’s salvation.]

The vices contrary to that general mutual duty of husbands and wives in furthering the #emph[salvation] of one another, and to the particular branches included under it, are many, such as

1\. #emph[A careless neglect of it:] when husbands and wives #footnote[1\. Care only for temporal things.] so mind earthly things that they think it enough if they are provident for one another in the things of this life. Most who live on this earth are guilty of this, and among others, even many of those who have the name of very good and kind husbands and wives. But whatever the opinion of others about them may be, the truth is that if they fail in this point, they go no further than the very heathen have done, and their kindness may be like the ape’s kindness, which causes death.

2\. #emph[The unworthy walk and unchristian conduct of a believer] #footnote[2\. Unworthy walking.] #emph[who is married to one who does not believe:] by this the unbeliever is kept from embracing the gospel, and made to dislike and detest it all the more. If a popish or profane husband is married to a wife who professes the truth of the gospel, and she is stubborn, proud, wanton, waspish, wasteful, or given to any other such vices, will he not be ready on that account to rail against the religion she professes, and utterly protest against it? So also a popish or profane wife, if she is married to such a husband.

3\. #emph[Negligence in observing one another’s disposition or conduct:] #footnote[3\. Negligence in preventing sin.] by which it comes about that they do not hold back or restrain one another from running into any sin, but show themselves such husbands and wives to one another as Eli showed himself a father to his sons. From this it came about that God’s severe vengeance fell on the neck of both the one and the other. Pilate’s wife, though a heathen woman, will rise up in judgment against many such wives, for she did what she could to keep #footnote[Matt 27:19.] her husband from shedding innocent blood.

4\. #emph[A flattering indulgence of one another’s whims,] and seeking #footnote[4\. Man-pleasing.] mutually to please one another in all things, without regard to good or evil. Scripture calls such people #footnote[Eph 6:6 ἀνθρωπάρεσκοι] #emph[men-pleasers.] So it comes about that husbands and wives are so far from drawing one another away from sin that the better rather yields to the worse, and both run into #footnote[#emph[Diabolus saepe sollicitus est subuertere viros per os coniugum.] \[The devil is often eager to overthrow husbands through the mouths of their wives.\] #emph[Orig. in Iob. lib.] 2.] evil: as Adam was persuaded by his wife to transgress against God’s express charge, and wise Solomon was drawn by his wives into idolatry, 1 Kgs 11:4, and Sapphira consented to the sacrilege of Ananias her husband, Acts 5:2.

5\. #emph[An undue fear of offending one another] by Christian instruction, #footnote[5\. Undue fear of offense.] admonition, reproof and the like. Many who are often moved in conscience to make known to their husbands and wives the sins in which they live, and the danger in which they lie because of them, nevertheless refrain and hold back from doing so through careless and causeless fear.

6\. #emph[An impious and envious disposition,] by which many husbands #footnote[6\. Scoffing at the signs of grace.] and wives are moved to mock and scoff at the holy zeal and eagerness which they observe in their bedfellows: as Michal, who likened David to a fool, or #footnote[2 Sam 6:20.] a vulgar fellow, because he showed his zeal by dancing before the ark. In this way many nip the work of the Spirit in the very bud, and cause grace soon to wither. But cursed be that husband or wife who thus perverts the main end of their close union.
